---
import CaseStudy from "../../layouts/CaseStudy.astro";

const metrics = [
  { value: "100+", label: "Staff positions audited" },
  { value: "15", label: "Outdated allocations found" },
  { value: "$40,000+", label: "Annual savings secured" },
  { value: "$9,000+", label: "Reimbursements recovered" },
];
---

<CaseStudy
  title="Fifteen positions funded against a department that had already changed"
  description="A hospital radiology budget that balanced every month for a year, and the reorganisations it had stopped describing. Case study by Linh (Min) Le."
  project="Department of Radiology, OSU Wexner Medical Center"
  period="June 2024 to May 2025"
  tint="finance"
  hook="The error was invisible precisely because nothing was broken. Payroll ran correctly, the ledger balanced, and the numbers behaved exactly as they were supposed to every month for a year."
  metrics={metrics}
>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2 class="h-sub">The decision this informed</h2>
        <p>
          A radiology department in an academic medical center is the operation behind every MRI, CT
          and ultrasound the hospital performs, and its budget reaches well past payroll. The money in
          those records supports the people running the department, the service contracts that keep
          imaging equipment working, and the supplies used in patient care.
        </p>
        <p>
          My work sat between the human resources system and the general ledger, which are two systems
          that describe the same department and are updated by different people on different schedules.
          The recurring deliverable was a monthly variance report to departmental leadership. The
          underlying job was making sure the numbers in it still described the department that
          actually existed.
        </p>
        <p class="section-note">
          The longer account of what that year taught me opens <a href="/">the front page</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2 class="h-sub">The finding</h2>
        <p>
          Auditing position allocations across <strong>more than a hundred</strong> clinical and
          administrative staff surfaced <strong>fifteen</strong> still funded at levels that three
          reorganisations had already changed. Roughly <strong>$40,000 a year</strong> was moving
          through the budget against an organisational structure that had stopped existing, and it had
          been doing so quietly enough that no monthly close had ever flagged it.
        </p>
        <p>
          Nothing about the ledger was wrong. Every allocation reconciled, every payroll run completed,
          and every month closed clean. A funding line pointing at a role that has been restructured
          still balances perfectly well, because balancing is a property of the arithmetic rather than
          of whether the arithmetic still describes anything real.
        </p>
        <p>
          Two adjacent audits came out of the same habit. Matching employee travel and clinical supply
          claims across three separate financial ledgers surfaced <strong>$9,000</strong> in
          reimbursements that had been approved and never paid, sitting in the gap between systems that
          each believed the other had handled it. Checking monthly invoices against purchase orders
          across <strong>more than fifty</strong> vendor contracts, covering over
          <strong>$300,000</strong> in annual departmental spend, surfaced unit price drift and
          quantity discrepancies that individually cleared any approval threshold and collectively did
          not.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2 class="h-sub">What I did, and the obvious alternative I rejected</h2>
        <p>
          The obvious way to check a budget is to reconcile it against itself, confirming that the
          totals tie and the variances have explanations. That process runs monthly in every finance
          department and it would never have found any of this, because an outdated allocation is
          internally consistent. It ties. It has an explanation. The explanation is simply describing a
          department that has since been reorganised.
        </p>
        <div class="pull">
          So the reconciliation ran against the operational reality instead of against the ledger.
          Position allocations were checked against who was actually doing which job, which turns the
          question from whether the numbers agree with each other into whether they agree with the
          department.
        </div>
        <p>
          That reframing is what makes the three findings versions of one finding. A stale position
          allocation, an unpaid reimbursement and a drifting unit price are all cases of two records
          that each look correct in isolation and stop agreeing once you put them side by side. The
          reimbursements were invisible to both ledgers individually and obvious across all three at
          once. The price drift was invisible per invoice and visible against the purchase order.
        </p>
        <p>
          The tooling was deliberately plain. An Excel reconciliation built on lookups and pivot tables
          across the three ledgers, designed so that the monthly comparison happened automatically
          rather than depending on whoever was doing the close that month remembering to look. The
          value was in defining what to compare, and the automation made sure the comparison kept
          happening after I had defined it.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2 class="h-sub">Assumptions and limitations</h2>
        <p>
          This page carries no exhibits and it cannot. The underlying records are hospital financial and
          personnel data, and nothing from them is reproducible outside the institution. Every figure
          here is stated at the level of aggregation that appears on my resume, which is the level I can
          stand behind publicly.
        </p>
        <p>
          The $40,000 is an annualised figure based on the corrected allocations at the point of
          correction. It represents funding redirected to the department's actual structure rather than
          cash recovered, and the two are worth distinguishing.
        </p>
        <p>
          The audit covered one department in one medical center over one year. Whether the same
          pattern sits in other departments is a reasonable inference and remains untested, and the
          drift itself was a consequence of reorganisation rather than of anything anyone did wrong.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

</CaseStudy>
